A||||12||1FST2M6||Throttle Body Bypass||||1||
Z||000000||1FST2M6||02-27-2000||06:37 PM||||I've seen a few people ask what a "throttle body bypass" was/is. There are two coolant lines that connect to the throttle body on a V6 car (don't know anything about the 4s... sorry guys)This is done to keep the throlle body from icing over and making your car difficult to start on sub 0 winter days. Well since I, BlackGT and Shaun too live where its nice we have completed this mod. The Coolant temps raises the intake air temperature thus decreasing the density of the air (more dense air= more power)  Disconnect these two coolant lines from the throttle body then follow them to just behind the water filler spout near your battery. disconnect the SAME two hoses (there may be some drainage but its only about 6oz. the smell will go away or rinse off the motor after your finished. OK after removing these two hoses (which just happen to be connected) take a piece of hose 3/8" in diameter and connect the two open tubes behind the water inlet spout. be sure to clamp these  VERY tight with  worm-screw type or a compression clamp of some sort. this tube will be subjected to pressures easily reaching 15psi. After this hose is connected to the two open tubes the throttle body will have 2 open inlet/dischare port don't worry about them... if you have to reattach the hoses you can... don't know why you'd ever want to though... Free HP. Lowers you intake temp by as much as 50*.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pretender||02-28-2000||01:31 AM||||Thanks, i've been wondering about that mod., Sounds very simple and hopefully it'll be done tomorrow weather permitting. thanks. shawn.||||reg||5
Z||000002||JIM GOOSE||02-28-2000||09:10 AM||||I disconnected the waterlines on my plenum some time ago with the same opinion as you,that is,not often below freezing here in GA. Later on, I removed the steel pipes that go to the throttle body in the interest of simplifying things. I used 5/16" rubber nipples to cap off the pipes on the waterneck. You can get two in apak for a dollar at Pep Boys.||||reg||12
Z||000003||1FST2M6||02-28-2000||10:36 AM||||Free or $.50 HP can't beat that! And its easy! With a dollar and an hour your done, and cleaned up!  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||02-28-2000||12:14 PM||||I'm in Wisconsin so probably not a good idea to permanantly disconnect. Anybody ever installed a valve?||||reg||5
Z||000005||1FST2M6||02-28-2000||01:50 PM||||A simple ball valve from like Home Depot or Ace would probly work just fine.. need to be Brass though.. galvanized steel or cast iron altho cheap would both oxidize and probly be REALLY heavy! Good idea!  just make sure its on the inlet side!||||reg||1
Z||000006||GREENDANO||02-28-2000||06:13 PM||||If you guys in the warm states get away with disconnecting your coolant tubes at the TB than I stand corrected.  But, I have seen carbs/throttle bodies ice up in 80 degree weather.  The difference is we have high humidity combined with the high velocity of the air flowing through the TB, which causes icing.  It has to do with the physics of bernuli's priciple.  <BR>Dan-O||||reg||3
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||02-28-2000||08:11 PM||||Assuming this was done on a Fiero only driven in the late spring/summer/early fall.. do you think there would be any problems?<P>Also.. anyone have any photos of this mod? Things like this would be perfect for my site..||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FST2M6||02-28-2000||10:45 PM||||Ok well this really doesn't sound taft or nice I didn't realize it till i reread it but it gets the point across so bear with me please don't ANYONE take offense. ((bernuli's priciple?... uum high pressure to low pressure in 10ths of a second causing condensation and temperature drop = icing... well yeah but we are not dealing with those kinds of extreme velocities. Maybe on a V8 with a tiny mal-engineered intake system but not our smaller 6s (sorry guys they are small, but size doesn't matter...)  I've had my car with this mod done to it since Sept 1st of 98 the same day i purshased it. I've driven it from 82k to 96k in ICE, snow, sleet and all kinds of other unfiero friendly condtions since that day with no problem. With the exception of one time it was POURING rain and i was running about 70mph, my K&N got wet.. it REFUSED to idle at the stop light when i got off the highway. But that is not related to this mod. The humidity he can go from less than 20% to rain in every temperature (coldest i've driven was about 18* actual air temp) So I can't vouch for minnesota I spent Christmas there it was -4* when i got off the plane and -11* when i got to Menahga so i'm not even REMOTELY familier with these temps on a car. Just that I haven't had any problems that were not self inflicted.))  I'm not aware of any problems with Shauns car or my friends Lumina Z34 we did the same thing too but mine seems ok... ||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||02-28-2000||11:37 PM||||The heating lines for the V6 are for exactly one purpose. To prevent Ice.<P>Icing isn't as big a problem in EFI as it is in a carb. For one thing the EFI can adjust for a certain amount of it.<P>Icing can and does happen in EFI. All EFI. Even the TBI on the 4 cyl can do it. Oddly the 4 cyl 700 TBI is most suseptable to in the idle air bypass. You'll know if it's doing it..... you'll get a code 45 that only happens in real cold weather, durring idle when the TBI gets cold. ((And no, this doesn't only happen right after a cold start........ A long drive at highway speed can also chill the 700 to where it will ice the IAC passage.))<P>For those of you with the V6. GM put the heater there for a reason. If there was no reason do you really think GM would spend a dime on an extra pipe? It's working for you because of luck as much as anything else. You've managed to avoid what ever condition(s) GM was worried about when they put it there.<P>The person(s) that thougth of putting a valve in had a good idea. That way you can turn it on if the weather does happen to screw you. The brass body ball valve was also good. try to make sure it's a stainless ball if possible.<P>One concern would be does the flow thru the TB affect engine cooling. You should check just where the coolant flow is comming and going. If it's even remotely posible that blocking the TB flow could block even a small part of block flow then you would want 2 valves. one to block the TB flow. another on a bypass tube so that when TB flow is off the block can still cool properly. seemingly small changes to block flow can have a major impact on cooling. I don't have access to a V6 so I can't check it. This is something I would want to know tho if I was doing this.<P>BTW. Relative humidity can be very low and you can still get icing.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Kage86GT4SP||02-29-2000||01:12 AM||||Actually, those lines on a 6 also make dandy coolant lines for the, uh, water-cooled turbo, say a TO 4?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||GREENDANO||02-29-2000||06:12 PM||||1FST2M6,<BR> Icing does occur.  Offended?  No, but I'm amazed what a little expirience does to opinions about things that NEVER happen.   Ogre, Thank-you.  I wasn't going to get into a long winded explanation, but I guess this thread warrented it.  <BR>  Dan-O||||reg||1
Z||000012||1FST2M6||02-29-2000||09:27 PM||||Not saying never just not to me or anyone i've had dealings with...   Had Icing issues on a Holley 900 (3 bore) on my previous car but I always thought and was told it was self-inflicted. To much beating on the car when i shouldn't have been...AND stupid me with no air filter...  is usually when my previous car would choke and die. ||||reg||1
